INT. PIE-BAKING CONTEST TENT - DAY

Gingham tablecloths. Fans stirring warm air. Judges' scorecards flutter in the draft.

CHLOE BANKS (30s, apron, flour on her cheek) lifts a lattice-topped pie and carries it to the judges, passing her rival HORACE PINKERTON (60s, chef's whites, ego like a thundercloud), who turns his own pie a precise quarter inch. Meanwhile the host, DOLORES FINCH (50s, sequin blazer), twirls her microphone cord. Two tables over, nine-year-old BIRDIE KAPOOR (9) licks a spoon, and her father RAJIV KAPOOR (40s) tries gently to take it away.

DOLORES
Contestants, five minutes!

HORACE
Five? We were promised ten!

CHLOE
We were not promised ten.

RAJIV
Birdie, spoon.

BIRDIE
It's evidence.

HORACE
Evidence of what?

BIRDIE
That your pie is dry.

Horace turns the color of rhubarb. Chloe sets her pie down and presses her lips together very hard.

DOLORES
Folks, folks, let's keep it friendly!

Horace seizes his tin and thrusts it forward, but the crust slides and his whole filling lands on the floor in a perfect round puddle. Nobody speaks. The fans whir.

BIRDIE
Told you.
